---
/**
 * Section — the shell every homepage and landing section sits in.
 *
 * WHY THIS EXISTS. "The spacing looks wrong" was reported against the homepage
 * repeatedly, and it was never one section being wrong. It was that no two
 * sections agreed on anything: the audit found five different H2 sizes (56,
 * 46.4, 43.2, 41.6 and 60.8px at 1440), six different max-widths, five gutters,
 * and section padding that ranged from 48px to 160px with no rule behind it.
 * Fixing that section by section is how it got that way. It is fixed once, here,
 * and every section inherits it.
 *
 * WHAT IT OWNS, AND THEREFORE WHAT A SECTION MUST NOT REDECLARE:
 *   - the measure and gutter (--measure / --gutter)
 *   - vertical padding (--sec-pad-y)
 *   - the head block: eyebrow, heading, standfirst, and the gaps between them
 *   - the gap from the head block down to the content (--sec-gap)
 *
 * A section that sets its own max-width, its own H2 size, or its own top
 * padding is reintroducing the defect. If a section genuinely needs to differ,
 * use `bleed` for full-width media and record the reason in the call site.
 *
 * CENTRED HEAD BLOCK — owner decision, 2026-08-02: "all the eyebrow must be
 * gradient" and every page "must be transformed for centrally aligned".
 *
 * This file previously carried the opposite instruction, on the grounds that the
 * legacy stylesheet forced sixteen element types to text-align:center inside
 * every section and then needed 138 per-element opt-outs across 24 pages to
 * escape. That history is real and it is the reason for the SCOPE of the rule
 * below, not a reason to ignore the decision.
 *
 * WHAT IS CENTRED: the head block only — eyebrow, heading, standfirst — plus the
 * eyebrow capsule itself. That is what every approved Figma render shows.
 *
 * WHAT IS NOT: the slotted body. A centring rule that reaches into `.section__body`
 * would hit cards, tables, matrix rows, code blocks and prose, and re-create the
 * exact opt-out cascade this file was written to end. Structured content keeps
 * its own internal alignment. If a specific body needs centring, it centres
 * itself at its own call site, where the reason is visible.
 */
interface Props {
  /** Small mono line above the heading. Use it for provenance, not decoration. */
  eyebrow?: string;
  /** The section heading. Omit for a section that is purely visual. */
  title?: string;
  /** One or two sentences under the heading. Kept to ~62ch for readability. */
  standfirst?: string;
  /**
   * Heading level. STRUCTURE ONLY — it never changes the rendered size, because
   * heading level is document structure and --t-h2 is the section-heading size.
   *
   * THE FIRST SECTION OF A PAGE MUST PASS as="h1". This was missing when the
   * union allowed only h2 and h3, and the result was four pages built on this
   * primitive (contact, faq, about, partners) that shipped with NO h1 at all,
   * while every hand-built page on the site had one. A page with no h1 has no
   * document title in the accessibility tree and loses its strongest on-page
   * ranking signal, and nothing in the markup looks wrong — which is exactly why
   * tests/heading-structure.spec.js now asserts exactly one h1 on every route.
   */
  as?: 'h1' | 'h2' | 'h3';
  /** Anchor id. Also used to wire aria-labelledby. */
  id?: string;
  /**
   * Content escapes the measure and runs the full viewport width. For media
   * stages and bands only; the head block always stays on the measure so the
   * text never detaches from the page's vertical rhythm.
   */
  bleed?: boolean;
  /**
   * This section CONTINUES the one above it rather than following it, so the
   * two share one gap instead of stacking their closing and opening space.
   *
   * Set it only where the previous section is the same surface and the same
   * kind of content — a register broken into groups, not a new subject. See the
   * argument beside `.section--joined` in this file's styles.
   */
  joined?: boolean;
  class?: string;
}

const {
  eyebrow,
  title,
  standfirst,
  as: Heading = 'h2',
  id,
  bleed = false,
  joined = false,
  class: className = '',
} = Astro.props;

/** Stable id for aria-labelledby, derived from the title when none is given. */
const slug =
  id ??
  (title
    ? title
        .toLowerCase()
        .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-')
        .replace(/^-|-$/g, '')
    : undefined);
const headingId = slug ? `${slug}-title` : undefined;
---

<section
  class:list={['section', { 'section--joined': joined }, className]}
  id={slug}
  aria-labelledby={title ? headingId : undefined}
>
  {
    (eyebrow || title || standfirst) && (
      <div class="section__head">
        {eyebrow && <p class="eyebrow">{eyebrow}</p>}
        {title && (
          <Heading class="section__title" id={headingId}>
            {title}
          </Heading>
        )}
        {standfirst && <p class="section__standfirst">{standfirst}</p>}
      </div>
    )
  }

  <div class:list={['section__body', { 'section__body--bleed': bleed }]}>
    <slot />
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .section {
    position: relative;
    padding-block: var(--sec-pad-y);
  }

  /*
   * ── O-43 · A SECTION THAT CONTINUES THE ONE ABOVE IT SHARES ITS GAP ──────
   *
   * WHAT IS BEING FIXED, MEASURED ON /faq AT 1440 BEFORE THIS RULE. The five
   * category blocks are five Sections in a row, so each seam is a closing
   * `--sec-pad-y` immediately followed by an opening one: 171px between the
   * last question of one category and the eyebrow of the next, four times. The
   * questions themselves are a ruled register on a ~60px rhythm, so the seam
   * reads as a hole roughly three times the height of anything around it.
   *
   * WHY THIS IS NOT A SITEWIDE CHANGE, AND THE NUMBER MATTERS. There are 61
   * adjacent same-surface Section pairs across 14 routes, so collapsing every
   * one of them would restate the vertical rhythm of a third of the site to
   * answer a report about one page. It is also not what the rest of them are:
   * /about's ten pairs are ten different subjects, and the room between them is
   * doing work.
   *
   * SO THE MODIFIER SAYS WHAT IS TRUE OF THE PAIR, NOT WHICH PAGE IT IS ON.
   * `joined` means "this is the same thing continuing" — one register broken
   * into groups. That is a claim about content, the call site is the only place
   * that knows it, and any page can make it. The alternative, a `#faq-products`
   * selector in a page's own styles, would be the patch this file's header
   * warns about: a section setting its own top padding.
   *
   * ZERO, NOT A SMALLER PADDING. The seam then IS the section rhythm — one
   * `--sec-pad-y`, the same distance the page already puts under its last
   * element — rather than a third value invented for this case. Measured after:
   * 171px to 85.5px at 1440x900, and to the token's 72px floor on a short
   * viewport.
   *
   * It cannot detach a heading from its own content: only the space ABOVE the
   * section moves, and `.section__head + .section__body` still owns the gap
   * inside it.
   */
  .section--joined {
    padding-block-start: 0;
  }

  /* The measure, in one place. Everything on the page lines up because every
     section's text starts on this same vertical. */
  .section__head,
  .section__body {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-inline: var(--gutter);
  }

  .section__body--bleed {
    max-width: none;
    padding-inline: 0;
  }

  /* Head block to content. The single gap that gives the page its rhythm. */
  .section__head + .section__body { margin-top: var(--sec-gap); }

  /* `.section__head { text-align: center }` (owner decision 2026-08-02) MOVED
     to styles/headings.css on 2026-08-03, with the eyebrow, the h1 treatment
     and the standfirst. PageHeader.astro emits the same head block for the 26
     routes that are not built from Section, and a rule that lives in one
     component's scoped styles cannot reach the other. See that file's header. */

  /*
   * THE EYEBROW IS A CAPSULE, and the capsule is doing structural work rather
   * than decoration. Once the head block is centred there is no left edge to
   * start the label against, so a bare line of mono floats. The outline gives it
   * a shape to sit in, and the gradient then has a defined width to resolve
   * across instead of running to the text box.
   *
   * This replaces the 56px rule that used to sit above the label. The rule was
   * the left-aligned answer to the same problem and reads as a stray dash once
   * the block is centred.
   *
   * THE COLOUR IS A SECOND SIGNAL, NEVER THE ONLY ONE. It is a mono,
   * letter-spaced, uppercase label inside a visible outline; remove all colour
   * and it still reads as an eyebrow. WCAG 1.4.1.
   *
   * background-clip: text needs a transparent fill, and an inherited
   * -webkit-text-fill-color is exactly what caused this site's invisible-text
   * P0. It is scoped to this one short label, @supports-gated so a browser
   * without it keeps a solid readable colour, and every route runs axe.
   */
  /* The eyebrow rules MOVED to styles/headings.css on 2026-08-03, and MOVED
     AGAIN to styles/labels.css on 2026-08-04. The reasoning above still applies
     and is repeated there; what changed is only where it lives.

     The second move is the interesting one. In headings.css the rule was written
     as `.section__head .section__eyebrow`, which still says an eyebrow only
     exists inside a head block — so the twenty-six pages that wanted the same
     label somewhere else still could not have it, and had each written their own.
     It is now `.eyebrow`, with no ancestor in the selector, which is why this
     component emits that class rather than a private one. */

  /*
   * THE FIRST SECTION OF A PAGE IS ITS HERO, AND HAS TO LOOK LIKE ONE.
   *
   * Owner, 2026-08-02: "hero section messages must be same size". Measured
   * across 24 routes, there were exactly two h1 sizes and the split had nothing
   * to do with editorial weight: 83.2px on every page with a hand-built hero
   * (home, blog, compare, glossary, sectors) and 46.4px on every page whose
   * hero comes from this component (about, contact, faq, partners, resources,
   * changelog, tools, crowmark, the legal pages, the calculator).
   *
   * The cause is the rule directly below. It sets every heading to --t-h2
   * because that is the SECTION heading size, and the `as` prop was documented
   * as structure-only so a page could pass as="h1" without changing size. That
   * is right for the second section on a page and wrong for the first, where
   * the same element is the page title.
   *
   * Sizing on the tag rather than adding a prop keeps `as` meaning exactly what
   * it says, and means no call site has to remember a second flag: a page that
   * declares its h1 here gets hero treatment because it declared an h1.
   *
   * ── AND IT DID NOT WORK, MEASURED 2026-08-04 ──────────────────────────────
   *
   * The h1 sized here at --t-h2 BEAT the h1 sized in styles/headings.css at
   * --t-h1, on every route built from this component, because Astro scopes a
   * component's styles with an attribute selector: `.section__title` shipped as
   * (0,2,0) and `h1.section__title` in the shared file is (0,1,1). So the
   * defect the note above describes was still live — 16 routes rendering their
   * page title at 46.4px while 26 rendered it at 83.2px, a 79% jump from one
   * route to the next, out of one class.
   *
   * `.section__title` — BOTH levels of it — now lives in styles/headings.css,
   * with the measurement written out beside it. Nothing about a section heading
   * is Section's private business: PageHeader.astro emits the same class on 26
   * routes that never load this file, which is the entire reason a component
   * cannot hold a sitewide treatment. Deleted here rather than lowered in
   * specificity, because a rule that only wins by outranking is a rule that will
   * be outranked back.
   */

  /* ── THE HEADING FADE, WHICH LIVE HAS AND WE DID NOT ────────────────────
   *
   * Owner, 2026-08-03, comparing crowagent.ai against localhost:8095. They were
   * right and I had been checking the wrong thing: I compared the ONE phrase
   * they first mentioned and confirmed it matched, while every heading on the
   * page differed.
   *
   * Read from the deployed site, live paints h1, h2 AND h3 with
   * `linear-gradient(#FFFFFF 0%, rgba(255,255,255,0.6) 100%)` clipped to the
   * text. Ours were flat --c-text. That is a difference on every heading of
   * every page, which is why the page read as "the gradient is not there".
   *
   * --grad-heading ALREADY HELD EXACTLY THAT VALUE and was referenced by
   * nothing. The token was ported across from live and never wired up, so the
   * design system recorded the intent and the pages ignored it. A token nobody
   * references is not a decision, it is a note.
   *
   * IT IS APPLIED WHERE THE COLOUR IS SET, NOT GLOBALLY, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
   * Astro's scoped component styles are unlayered, so a blanket `h1, h2, h3`
   * rule in a stylesheet would lose to every component that sets `color` on its
   * own heading. Applying it beside the colour it replaces is the only place it
   * reliably wins.
   *
   * THE FALLBACK IS THE SOLID COLOUR ABOVE, kept rather than removed, so a
   * browser without background-clip and forced-colours mode both get readable
   * text. `-webkit-text-fill-color: transparent` INHERITS, which is exactly how
   * this codebase produced invisible text once before, so anything nested that
   * needs its own colour must set it. */
  /* ── h1 ONLY, CORRECTED 2026-08-03 AFTER LOOKING AT IT ────────────────
   *
   * This was `.section__title`, which is every section heading on the site.
   * Two things were wrong with that, and only one was visible in a measurement.
   *
   * MEASURED: the deployed site paints the fade on its hero h1 and NOT on its
   * section h2s, which report `background-image: none`. I read the gradient off
   * one unclassed h2 in an earlier pass and generalised from it.
   *
   * SEEN: the fade runs top to bottom over the element, so on a heading that
   * wraps to two lines the SECOND LINE renders at the 60% end. "Delivery is the
   * first stage of the next bid." had a bright first line and a visibly grey
   * second one. On a single-line hero it reads as light; on a wrapped section
   * heading it reads as a fault.
   *
   * Worth recording that no gate caught it and no number would have: contrast
   * still passes at 60% white, the token was correct, the tier was correct.
   * It took looking at the page. */
  /* EVERY h1.section__title RULE, AND THE STANDFIRST, MOVED to
     styles/headings.css on 2026-08-03 — the shine, the per-line tiling, the
     forced-colours reset, the h1 sizing and the 62ch lede measure.

     The history above is kept because it records two real defects and how they
     were found, but the rules themselves cannot live here. Measured on the day:
     26 of 43 routes rendered a flat white h1, because blog posts, legal pages,
     sector pages, comparison pages and three index pages do not use Section and
     so could never reach a rule in Section's scoped styles. They had each grown
     their own header instead — .cmp-h1, .legal__title, .gl-h1 and an unclassed
     h1 on fifteen routes — which the owner reported as "why so many different
     style?".

     What remains here is `.section__title` above, which is the h2 section
     heading and genuinely is Section's own business. The PAGE title is not. */
</style>
